    "BUNGEE JUMPING (BUNGY JUMPING in New Zealand/Australia) is an EXTREME SPORT + ADVENTURE ACTIVITY in which a person JUMPS from a HIGH STRUCTURE (bridge, crane, tower, or natural feature) while ATTACHED to a LARGE ELASTIC CORD (bungee/bungy cord) that stretches to absorb the fall energy + rebounds the jumper up multiple times before settling. HISTORICAL ORIGIN: LAND DIVING (NAGHOL) ritual of PENTECOST ISLAND, VANUATU — young men jumped from wooden towers ~20-30m tall with LIANA VINES tied to their ankles as a coming-of-age + yam harvest ritual dating back centuries. MODERN SPORT: Filmed by BBC 1970s + inspired the OXFORD UNIVERSITY DANGEROUS SPORTS CLUB — DAVID KIRKE, CHRIS BAKER, ALAN WESTON + others made the FIRST MODERN BUNGEE JUMPS from the CLIFTON SUSPENSION BRIDGE in BRISTOL, ENGLAND on 1 APRIL 1979 (245ft/75m). They wore top hats + tails; were arrested but released without charge. COMMERCIALIZATION: NEW ZEALANDER A.J. HACKETT + HENRY VAN ASCH developed COMMERCIAL BUNGEE JUMPING. Hackett made a famous test jump from the EIFFEL TOWER in JUNE 1987. On 12 NOVEMBER 1988 they opened the WORLD'S FIRST COMMERCIAL BUNGEE SITE at KAWARAU BRIDGE, QUEENSTOWN, NEW ZEALAND (43m/141ft) — the birthplace of commercial bungee. QUEENSTOWN became the ADVENTURE CAPITAL OF THE WORLD. TECHNIQUE: JUMPER wears BODY HARNESS (traditional) or ANKLE HARNESS (traditional Kiwi style) attached to elastic BUNGEE CORD anchored above. Cord chosen based on JUMPER'S WEIGHT (5 weight categories typical); STRETCHES 4-5 TIMES ITS RESTING LENGTH; jumper falls 3-5 seconds free-fall then bounces UP-DOWN multiple times before settling. WATER TOUCH options (dunk head/hands in river below). LOWERED BY OPERATOR onto raft or ground. SAFETY: BACKUP LINES + double-check every attachment; FALL ARREST SYSTEM; PROFESSIONAL OPERATORS regulated by national adventure tourism standards. INCIDENT RATE remarkably low (1 in ~500,000 jumps at reputable sites) but SPECTACULAR failures do occur (cord miscalculation, harness detachment). VARIATIONS: (1) STANDARD BUNGEE — head-first free-fall + rebound. (2) BUNGEE SWING / CATAPULT — reverse direction (fired UP from ground by tension). (3) BUNGEE ROCKET — 2-person seated launch. (4) SCAD DIVING — free-fall into airbag (no cord). (5) NAGHOL — traditional Vanuatu vine jumping (dangerous, still practiced). ICONIC BUNGEE SITES: KAWARAU BRIDGE (43m, NZ) — world's first commercial; NEVIS HIGHWIRE (134m, NZ) — highest in NZ; MACAU TOWER (233m, Macau) — HIGHEST COMMERCIAL BUNGEE IN THE WORLD (AJ Hackett); VERZASCA DAM (220m, Switzerland) — Made famous by JAMES BOND: GOLDENEYE opening (1995 Pierce Brosnan); ROYAL GORGE BRIDGE (321m, USA — highest suspension bridge bungee); BLOUKRANS BRIDGE (216m, South Africa — highest bridge bungee); VICTORIA FALLS BRIDGE (111m, Zambia-Zimbabwe border). NO GOVERNING FEDERATION — regulated by adventure tourism boards (Adventure Tourism Council, national governments); INTERNATIONAL BUNGEE JUMPING ASSOCIATION exists but limited authority; INDUSTRY STANDARDS via IATA, CTS, EN 15567 (Europe). MOST JUMPS RECORD: JOHN 'BIG JOHN' CULHANE (12,000+ jumps); DAVE BARLIA (10,000+). Featured in extreme sports culture; adventure tourism cornerstone; ~2 MILLION people bungee jump annually worldwide.",
